## 2. Preserve the core architecture

The three JSON files remain the policy corpus:

```text
sources.json
rules.json
candidate-updates.json
```

Maintain these rules:

- Only verified rules may generate findings.
- Candidate updates must never activate logic.
- Every important student-facing policy statement must trace to a verified source.
- Missing information must result in an insufficient-information state.
- Never guess a date or legal outcome.
- Publication date and effective date must remain distinct.
- Visa expiration, I-20 program end, EAD expiration and I-94 admit-until date must remain distinct.
- The same profile, corpus and `asOfDate` must always produce the same deterministic evaluation.
- AI must never modify or override the rules-engine output.

Do not change a legal date, threshold or rule merely because it appears suspicious. Record it in `docs/POLICY_AUDIT.md` unless an authoritative primary source supports the correction.

Pay particular attention to:

- September 15, 2026 effective-status gating;
- existing D/S students;
- fixed-date admissions;
- transition-period calculations;
- I-20 program dates;
- OPT and STEM OPT windows;
- travel and readmission;
- grace/departure periods;
- pending, delayed, stayed, enjoined, terminated or uncertain rules;
- hard-coded dates such as the transition cap;
- date boundary behavior;
- self-reported `maintainingStatus`.

Do not treat the student’s answer to “Are you maintaining status?” as an authoritative status determination. Improve the wording or logic so it is presented as information requiring DSO confirmation.

